Run touch flings on Flutter's scroll physics: one `ScrollPhysics` type for flings, overscroll and the platform table.

What: ports Flutter 3.50.1's physics (flutter/flutter ae9b586f34; names, constants and comments kept) into
`gestures/`, one Rust type per Flutter class:
- `gestures/physics.rs` (`gpui::physics`): physics/*.dart in one module: `Tolerance`, `Simulation`,
  `FrictionSimulation`, `SpringSimulation` and `ScrollSpringSimulation`, `near_equal` / `near_zero`, and
  foundation's `precisionErrorTolerance` (lsq_solver.rs now reads it from here). Springs run on GPUI's own
  `SpringConfig` (`SpringDescription` is `SpringConfig`, plus `SpringConfig::with_damping_ratio`), because it passes
  every spring case of Flutter's newton_test.dart (spring_types, crit_spring, overdamped_spring, underdamped_spring,
  and through `BouncingScrollSimulation` test_kinetic_scroll and over/under scroll spring) and the rubber-band rows
  of scroll_physics_test.dart; `SpringSimulation` is glue over `SpringConfig::step` with Flutter's `isDone`, and
  Flutter's `_SpringSolution` classes are not ported.
- `gestures/scroll_simulation.rs`: widgets/scroll_simulation.dart, `BouncingScrollSimulation` (friction drag 0.135,
  UIScrollView's 0.998 per ms, written once) and `ClampingScrollSimulation` (Android's `OverScroller`, made
  ballistic; Flutter's fixed 160 logical pixels per inch).
- `gestures/scroll_physics.rs`: widgets/scroll_physics.dart. GPUI's public `ScrollPhysics` becomes Flutter's class
  with a variant per subclass: `Bouncing { deceleration_rate: ScrollDecelerationRate }` (`Normal`, or macOS's `Fast`
  with its 1400 constant deceleration and spring) and `Clamping`, replacing upstream's `Exponential { decay_per_ms }`
  and `FrictionSpline { friction, pixels_per_inch }`. `ios()` and `android()` keep their names (Bouncing normal,
  Clamping), so gpui_web's user-agent rows compile unchanged. Its methods are Flutter's: `spring`,
  `drag_start_distance_motion_threshold`, `apply_physics_to_user_offset` (overscroll friction),
  `apply_boundary_conditions`, `create_ballistic_simulation(metrics, velocity)`, with `ScrollMetrics`,
  `tolerance_for(device_pixel_ratio)` and `ScrollDecelerationRate::friction_factor`.
The recognizer's momentum runs `create_ballistic_simulation` with no extents (the scroll container clamps) at
`tolerance_for(2.0)` (it doesn't know the window's ratio yet); `Momentum` ends on the first tick its simulation is
done at, as Flutter's ballistic scroll does. `MOMENTUM_STOP_VELOCITY` and the transcribed AOSP `friction_spline`
module and its table test go; the "fling curves are sane" test runs on the new simulations. Flutter's tests are
ported as tables, one row per Flutter test: all of friction_simulation_test.dart, scroll_simulation_test.dart, the
friction, spring and bouncing cases of newton_test.dart, and scroll_physics_test.dart's BouncingScrollPhysics /
ClampingScrollPhysics maths (one "doesn't alter the velocity for time 0" row over both physics).

Why: Flutter's tests fail on the old maths. Android's fling was AOSP's `SplineOverScroller` curve, which Flutter
replaced because it is not ballistic (flutter/flutter#113424, #120338): its starting velocity is not the release
velocity (2863.6 px/s for a 2866.9 px/s release), it stops with a jump (from 4.9 px/s to 0; a 187 px/s step in one
frame against the test's 85.5 bound), and a fling restarted from its own position and velocity does not retrace it.
iOS used Flutter's friction law with no `Fast` rate, so macOS flings could not decelerate as Flutter's do. Keeping
GPUI's two-preset enum next to Flutter's physics classes would have held each platform's scroll feel twice (fling,
overscroll, platform table); one type holds it once. Not platform-specific, so no cfg.

Upstream: not filed (upstreaming paused, AGENTS.md patch rule 14).

